.TH PAWELI 1 "August 2026" "paweli" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paweli \- run and compile paweli programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paweli
.RI [ option ... ]
.I file
.br
.B paweli
.B init
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B paweli
.B run
.RI [ file.pw ]
.RI [ option ... ]
.br
.B paweli
.B test
.RI [ file.pw ]
.RI [ option ... ]
.br
.B paweli
.B build
.I file.pw
.RI [ output.paw ]
.RI [ option ... ]
.br
.B paweli
.B build
.I file.pw
.B \-\-bin
.I name
.RI [ option ... ]
.br
.B paweli
.B build
.RI [ option ... ]
.br
.B paweli
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paweli
is a small, statically-typed, C-like programming language. Programs
compile to bytecode and run on a stack-based VM with a JIT compiler for
hot loops and functions. A program is a single
.I .pw
file and needs a top-level
.B main
function, the same as C; every top-level function is defined first, then
.B main()
is called.
.PP
.I paweli
.I file
runs a program. Given a
.I .pw
source file, it parses, compiles, and runs it. Given a
.I .paw
file (a compiled bytecode container, detected by its magic bytes
regardless of extension), it skips straight to running the already\-
compiled program, with no parsing or compiling step.
.PP
.B paweli run
does the same thing as
.IR "paweli file" ,
just as its own subcommand rather than a bare invocation: given
.IR file.pw ,
it runs it directly. Given no
.I file.pw
argument, it reads
.B entry
out of
.B project.pawproj
in the current directory instead. See
.B PROJECTS
below.
.PP
.B paweli test
is the same as
.B run
but always runs every
.B @test
\-tagged function instead of
.BR main() .
This replaces the old run-time
.B \-\-run-tests
flag entirely; there is no flag for this anymore, only this
subcommand.
.B build \-\-run-tests
(a different, build-time flag, unaffected by this) still bakes test
mode into a compiled artifact; see below.
.PP
.B paweli build
compiles a
.I .pw
file (and the
.B #module
it depends on, if any) without running it, for distributing a program
as one file. Two output kinds:
.IP \(bu 2
.B .paw
(default): a compiled bytecode container. The output path is an optional
second argument and defaults to the input path with its
.I .pw
extension replaced by
.I .paw
(or with
.I .paw
appended if the input does not end in
.IR .pw ).
Still needs
.B paweli
installed to run
.RI ( "paweli out.paw" ).
.IP \(bu 2
A native executable
.RB ( \-\-bin\ \c
.IR name ):
a single, directly-runnable file; a copy of the currently running
.B paweli
binary with the compiled program appended, so it carries its own
runtime with it. See
.B "NATIVE EXECUTABLES"
below.
.PP
Called with no
.I file.pw
argument at all,
.B paweli build
reads a
.B project.pawproj
manifest from the current directory instead, and writes its output
under
.B build/
rather than beside the source. See
.B PROJECTS
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Initializing a project"
.B paweli init
.RI [ name ]
writes
.B project.pawproj
and a starter
.B main.pw
into the current directory. See
.B PROJECTS
below.
.SS "Running or testing a program"
.TP
.B \-\-dump-ast
Prints the parsed AST to stdout before compiling. Only meaningful for a
.I .pw
file; has no effect (with a warning) on a
.I .paw
file or built executable, since there is no source to parse.
.TP
.B \-\-dump-bytecode
Prints disassembled bytecode for every compiled function to stdout,
before running. Works for a
.I .pw
file, a
.I .paw
file, and a built executable.
.TP
.B \-\-jit-stats
Prints each function's JIT state (compiled, failed, or not compiled)
and hotness counter to stdout after the run finishes.
.PP
.B paweli test
runs every
.B @test
\-tagged top-level function instead of
.BR main()
.RB ( main()
must still exist, it just is not called), same as
.B run
in every other respect. Only meaningful for a
.I .pw
file; a
.I .paw
file's or built executable's run mode was already fixed at
.B build
time.
.TP
.B \-\-stack-size\ \c
.I N
Sets the VM's value stack size, in slots. Default
.BR 4096 ,
minimum
.BR 16 .
.TP
.B \-\-max-frames\ \c
.I N
Sets the maximum call-frame depth (how deep function calls can nest,
including recursion). Default
.BR 256 ,
minimum
.BR 1 .
.PP
Both
.B \-\-dump-ast
and
.B \-\-dump-bytecode
still run the program afterward, unless parsing or compiling failed.
.B \-\-stack-size ,
.B \-\-max-frames ,
.B \-\-dump-bytecode ,
and
.B \-\-jit-stats
work the same way on a built executable, passed directly to it
.RB ( "./myprogram \-\-stack-size 8192" ).
.SS "Building a program"
.TP
.BI output.paw
Optional second positional argument: where to write the compiled
.I .paw
file. Defaults to the input path with its
.I .pw
extension replaced by
.I .paw .
Not used with
.BR \-\-bin ,
which names its own output.
.TP
.B \-\-bin\ \c
.I name
Build a native executable named
.I name
instead of a
.I .paw
file. See
.B "NATIVE EXECUTABLES"
below.
.TP
.B \-\-strip-source
Omits the embedded source text from the output. Produces a smaller
file; a runtime error from the result still prints its
.RB "[" code "]"
.I file:line:
message and call stack, just without the source-line snippet under it.
Source is embedded by default.
.TP
.B \-\-run-tests
Bakes test mode into the compiled program: running the result runs
every
.B @test
\-tagged function instead of
.BR main() ,
the same as
.B paweli test
for the equivalent
.I .pw
file. This is the one place
.B \-\-run-tests
still exists as a flag; the run-time version was replaced entirely by
the
.B paweli test
subcommand (see above), since baking a fixed run mode into a
.I compiled
artifact is a genuinely different thing from choosing one for a single
run.
.PP
A build bundles the entry program and its one
.B #module
dependency (if any) into a single output; a
.B #moduledef
file can never itself have a
.B #module
import, so there is never more than one to bundle.
.PP
Called with no
.I file.pw
argument, this reads
.B entry
out of
.B project.pawproj
in the current directory instead, and writes under
.B build/
rather than beside the source. See
.B PROJECTS
below.
.SS Version
.TP
.B \-\-version ", " \-v
Prints the installed
.B paweli
version and exits, without a file argument.
.SS "No arguments"
Running
.B paweli
with no file and no subcommand prints a help banner instead of an error
one\-liner: the version, the usage forms from
.B SYNOPSIS
above, two example invocations, the run\-time options, and a pointer to
this manual. Exit status is still
.BR 1 ,
same as any other missing\-argument error.
.SH PROJECTS
.RS
.nf
mkdir myapp && cd myapp
paweli init
paweli run
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B paweli init
writes
.B project.pawproj
and a starter
.B main.pw
into the current directory. It refuses to run if
.B project.pawproj
is already there, rather than overwrite it;
.B main.pw
is only written if it does not already exist. An optional
.I name
argument both names the project and creates a new directory of that
name to scaffold into (tolerating it already existing); without one,
.B init
scaffolds directly into the current directory instead, and the name
defaults to that directory's own name.
.PP
.B project.pawproj
reuses paweli's own lexical syntax rather than a separate format like
TOML, though it is not itself a paweli program. It looks like this:
.RS
.nf
string name = "myapp";
string version = "0.1.0";
string entry = "main.pw";
.fi
.RE
Only
.RI "string " field " = " "\(dqvalue\(dq;"
declarations are recognized, in any order, and all three fields above
.RB ( name ,
.BR version ,
and
.BR entry )
are required.
.PP
There is no dependency list. A project's file set is not declared in
the manifest at all: it is whatever
.B entry
and its
.B #module
/
.B #moduledef
graph transitively reach, the same as for a single-file program.
Fetching or versioning someone else's code is not supported yet.
.PP
.B paweli run
and
.B paweli test
with no
.I file.pw
argument read
.B entry
out of
.B project.pawproj
in the current directory and run it directly, same as
.B "paweli run main.pw"
(or
.BR "paweli test main.pw" )
would. Neither writes any output file.
.PP
.B paweli build
with no
.I file.pw
argument reads
.B entry
out of
.B project.pawproj
in the current directory and compiles it, same as
.B "paweli build main.pw"
would. The only difference is where the output goes:
.B build/<name>.paw
(or
.B build/<name>
for
.BR \-\-bin )
instead of beside the source.
.B build/
is created automatically.
.SH "NATIVE EXECUTABLES"
.RS
.nf
paweli build hello.pw \-\-bin hello
./hello
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-bin
produces a self-contained, directly-runnable file: a copy of the
.B paweli
binary that ran the
.B build
command, with the compiled program appended after it, followed by a
small trailer recording where that program starts. On startup, every
.B paweli
invocation checks its own executable file for that trailer before doing
anything else; an ordinary
.B paweli
binary (nothing appended) never matches, so this adds no visible
behavior to normal use.
.PP
This means the executable still carries the whole paweli runtime (VM,
garbage collector) inside it, not just the compiled program. It is not
an ahead-of-time-compiled native binary the way a C or Rust program is,
and its size and startup cost reflect that (from a few hundred KB to a
few MB, depending on which
.B paweli
build produced it, plus whatever the compiled program itself adds).
.PP
Which
.B paweli
binary you run
.B build \-\-bin
from matters: a Debug build (AddressSanitizer, optimizer off, unstripped
symbols) makes a correspondingly large, slow executable; a Release build
makes a small, fast one. There is no cross-compilation, and a binary
only runs on the same OS and architecture that built it. On Windows the
output file gets a
.B .exe
suffix.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to any non-empty value to disable colored diagnostic output. Color
is otherwise on automatically when stderr is a terminal.
.TP
.B PAWELI_GC_STRESS
Set to any non-empty value to run a full garbage-collection cycle before
every single instruction, instead of only when the heap grows past its
threshold. Much slower; meant for shaking out GC correctness bugs.
.TP
.B PAWELI_GC_DEBUG
Set to any non-empty value to print each collection's byte counts
(before, after, and the threshold that triggered it) to stderr.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Parsing, compiling, or running failed, or the command line was used
incorrectly.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every diagnostic, from the lexer, parser, compiler, or VM, is reported
in one shared format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[code] file:line:col: error: message
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I code
is a stable, namespaced slug
.RB ( "parser::expected-token" ,
.BR "compiler::duplicate-local" ,
.BR "vm::division-by-zero" ,
\&...) that identifies the kind of error independent of the message
text. The stage prefix
.RB ( "lexer::" ,
.BR "parser::" ,
.BR "compiler::" ,
.BR "vm::" )
tells you which stage raised it. Most diagnostics are
.BR error ,
which stop the program; a few are
.BR warning ,
which print the same way but never stop compilation.
.PP
Runtime errors print the failing message with its own
.RB "[" code "]"
.I file:line:
.BR "error:"\  message
header, then a call-stack trace from the point of failure outward:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[vm::undefined-variable] example.pw:10: error: undefined variable
example.pw:10: in main()
example.pw:1: in <script>
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B <script>
is the synthetic top-level frame that defines every function and calls
.BR main() .
.PP
Two stack-related errors are distinct:
.IP \(bu 2
.B vm::stack-overflow
.RI ( "stack overflow" ):
the value stack exceeded
.BR \-\-stack-size .
.IP \(bu 2
.B vm::call-stack-overflow
.RI ( "call stack overflow" ):
call nesting exceeded
.BR \-\-max-frames ,
usually from unbounded recursion.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program from source:
.RS
.nf
paweli hello.pw
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile a program to a bytecode file:
.RS
.nf
paweli build hello.pw hello.paw
paweli hello.paw
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile a program to a native executable:
.RS
.nf
paweli build hello.pw \-\-bin hello
./hello
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B paweli
runs on x86_64 Linux and x86_64 Windows. macOS and ARM support is planned.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I file.pw
Paweli source file.
.TP
.I file.paw
Compiled bytecode container, run with
.RB "paweli file.paw" .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR paweli-syntax (7)
.PP
.B paweli
itself is licensed under GPLv3. Programs written in paweli are not; the
runtime exception (see
.I RUNTIME-EXCEPTION
in the source tree) covers
.I .paw
files and
.B \-\-bin
executables, so closed-source paweli programs are fine to write and
distribute.
